[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124472,
      "id": "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30",
      "title": "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a88afe90-2dc0-542b-a35d-32c66e08e046",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/5693b736370a762704ae28d6ed3f3dfc54529a48712b17b64630b687de1dcda8.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nEach graph shows its entire domain; an open circle excludes its endpoint.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/c1012603b288664ec16efe13a8311e9daf9f8950d096dbe529b61a161abe7a90.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graphs I, III, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, these graphs show one-to-one functions. As a result, these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graphs II, IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "fcd4e5a7-325d-5b95-981e-4945fa5de434",
