    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0b348bc5-c859-5fc4-b505-c63d75ea49d1",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/dbaabb5ef72658d231bcc054c5c6a098455a80f96d816c17ae0c6029e41ad85e.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nEach graph shows its entire domain; an open circle excludes its endpoint.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/40100cd86e6f8ea5f46dc7af179b9889bc1881eede40ddf80531e4a8192f0807.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graphs II, IV, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, these graphs show one-to-one functions. As a result, these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
